  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A mid-level shortwave will lift slowly \nnortheast along the coast of the Carolinas this morning, then \nshift away from the coast this afternoon. An attendant area of \nlow pressure will also lift northeast along the coast while \npulling a warm front north with it. The SFC low and warm front \nare expected to provide an area of enhanced low-level \nconvergence along their trek up the coast. How close to the \ncoast the low travels, and how much instability can be realized,\nwill be important factors for which areas see the heaviest \nrainfall rates today. Given how convectively overturned the \nairmass is from yesterday, it may be that the instability \ngradient will reside right along the immediate coast, which \nwould favor areas from coastal Onslow northeast through the OBX \nfor the heaviest rainfall today. It's also possible that the \ninstability gradient remains just offshore, keeping the higher \nrainfall rates offshore as well. Regardless, the heaviest \nrainfall axis should reside east of where the heaviest rainfall \naxis was yesterday, and east of where the Flood Watch originally\ncovered. In light of that, the Flood Watch has been cancelled, \nand a watch is not currently anticipated for the coastal areas \ntoday due to 1) sandy soils which can be harder to flood, and 2)\nuncertainty regarding whether or not the higher instability \nwill develop along the coast or offshore.\n\nFurther inland, showers and scattered thunderstorms remain \npossible, but rainfall rates should be lower than yesterday due \nto less instability. However, some instances of minor poor \ndrainage flooding may still occur. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An anomalously strong upper level trough is \nforecast to translate east from the Great Lakes to the Northeast\nU.S. Sunday and Monday. Meanwhile, an associated strong cold \nfront is forecast to push east across the Mid-Atlantic and \nCarolinas with much drier air in tow behind it. Normally this \nwould spell a reduced risk of thunderstorms for ENC, and for \nmost areas it probably will. However, recent guidance has \nstarted to trend slower with the front's eastward progression as\nthe upper level forcing shifts NE away from the region, leaving\nthe tail end of the front to potentially stall near the coast \nof the Carolinas. There appears to be 2 possible scenarios at \nthis point, each of which is outlined below.\n\nSCENARIO 1: The front clears the coast of ENC. This scenario \nfavors a reduced risk of convection areawide along with slightly\nbelow normal overnight lows and lower humidity. This scenario \nwas previously favored, but has recently lost some model \nsupport. \n\nSCENARIO 2: The front makes it to the coast, but then stalls. \nIn this scenario, dry conditions are favored inland, but with a \nrisk of thunderstorms lingering along the coast. Interestingly, \nthis scenario offers the potential for a sneaky strong to severe\nthunderstorm risk as drier mid-level air becomes more \nsupportive of damaging downbursts. Lift and shear could also be \nenhanced as the area would remain in the entrance region of a \nstrong upper jet. Lift could also be enhanced if a surface low \nrides up along the stalled front. Machine learning guidance do \nnot show much of a signal for strong to severe convection, while\nthe ECMWF's EFI guidance does show a decent signal. This \nscenario has been gaining some support in recent runs. \n\nFor now, much of ENC is likely to remain dry Sunday and Monday,\nbut for coastal areas, the thunderstorm risk is something worth\nkeeping an eye on.",
